{"step_index":85,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T06:44:02Z","content":"What you are describing is called **Auto-fill / In-Page Helper** (just like Bitwarden or 1Password)!\n\nHere is how that works and why a regular web page cannot do it alone:\n\n---\n\n### Why a normal web page can't do this:\nFor your safety, web browsers have a strict security rule called the **Browser Sandbox (Same-Origin Policy)**:\n* A webpage running in one tab (like our password manager) is **forbidden** from reading, touching, or modifying any other tab (like `github.com`).\n* If browsers allowed regular web pages to inject buttons or read inputs on other websites, hackers could create a webpage to spy on your bank or email tabs!\n\n---\n\n### The Solution: A Browser Extension (Chrome / Edge)\nTo show a copy/auto-fill button directly inside GitHub's login page, you need a **Browser Extension**. \n\nA browser extension has special permissions to:\n1. Detect when you are on `github.com`.\n2. Look up your encrypted GitHub password in your local vault.\n3. Place a small shield icon or popup right inside the GitHub username & password boxes to **auto-fill or copy with 1 click**.\n4. Still stay **100% offline** (no cloud, no tracking, pure local AES-256 encryption).\n\n---\n\n### Would you like me to build this as a Chrome/Edge Extension for you?\nI can create a complete browser extension folder that you can load into Chrome or Edge in 30 seconds using **Developer Mode** (`Load unpacked`). \n\nIt will have:\n* The same offline encrypted vault.\n* An auto-fill icon that pops up directly on websites like GitHub, Google, etc.\n\nLet me know if you would like me to create this for you!","thinking":"**Interpreting User's Autofill Request**\n\nThe user's goal involves a seamless autofill mechanism or an in-page interface on an external site like GitHub. This implies a need for a system to interact with the external website, possibly through an overlay or a similar user interface, to automate the password copying. The design should facilitate automatic password retrieval when the user visits the relevant external website.\n\n\n**Evaluating Browser Extension Feasibility**\n\nA standard webpage's security restrictions prevent direct interaction with other websites; therefore, a browser extension is required. The functionalities needed, such as injecting an autofill interface, are achievable through a browser extension, similar to existing password managers. This approach allows an offline React/HTML application to serve as the user interface within a secured extension environment.\n\n\n"}
